1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
You have this great business idea,
and your friends all say it's great.

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:06,240
That's kind, but a friend's
yes costs them nothing,

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,140
so it can't tell you
if strangers will pay.

4
00:00:08,765 --> 00:00:11,025
So then comes the question,
what does tell you?

5
00:00:11,225 --> 00:00:12,665
Before you spend months building it?

6
00:00:13,205 --> 00:00:15,345
Most people assume you have
to go and talk to strangers.

7
00:00:16,025 --> 00:00:18,425
And while there is truth to
that, if you are like me,

8
00:00:18,665 --> 00:00:20,085
that sounds awkward and slow.

9
00:00:20,525 --> 00:00:21,625
You can start without that.

10
00:00:22,045 --> 00:00:25,345
In most cases, buyers of similar
products have already left evidence,

11
00:00:25,725 --> 00:00:27,925
and the best part is,
AI can read it for you.

12
00:00:28,476 --> 00:00:31,296
Later in this video I will take
you through this free AI tool,

13
00:00:31,696 --> 00:00:35,416
the Riskiest Guess Finder, it finds
and ranks the guesses an idea rests

14
00:00:35,436 --> 00:00:37,516
on and writes the cheapest test for it.

15
00:00:37,996 --> 00:00:40,956
Here it is on an example idea,
a series of KDP planners,

16
00:00:41,456 --> 00:00:42,656
and you can also run it on yours.

17
00:00:43,599 --> 00:00:46,379
Just to show you, here's
what building first can cost.

18
00:00:47,039 --> 00:00:49,019
Eric Ries co-founded
a company called IMVU,

19
00:00:49,319 --> 00:00:52,919
and in a 2010 talk he said his team
spent about six months building an add-on

20
00:00:52,939 --> 00:00:54,039
for instant-messaging apps.

21
00:00:54,501 --> 00:00:58,281
Then in his 2009 interview he described
a tester who wouldn't even invite

22
00:00:58,301 --> 00:00:59,561
a friend to use the feature.

23
00:01:00,021 --> 00:01:02,301
The idea didn't hold up, and in his words

24
00:01:02,321 --> 00:01:04,581
they wound up having to
throw all of that code away.

25
00:01:04,929 --> 00:01:06,769
Would anyone even have
clicked a download button?

26
00:01:07,309 --> 00:01:09,209
His own answer was basically no.

27
00:01:09,789 --> 00:01:11,629
That six months wasted, taught what

28
00:01:11,649 --> 00:01:13,648
a cheaper test could have
shown from the start.

29
00:01:13,908 --> 00:01:17,568
Your idea runs the same risk, and the
belief behind it is usually that if

30
00:01:17,588 --> 00:01:18,988
people like it, it's wanted.

31
00:01:19,568 --> 00:01:20,948
But liking costs them nothing.

32
00:01:21,408 --> 00:01:25,928
Your idea is a prototype: test it on
real customers before you build it.

33
00:01:26,441 --> 00:01:30,521
What guess does your idea rest on,
how can AI get you evidence before you

34
00:01:30,541 --> 00:01:32,721
build, and which test
should you run this week.

35
00:01:33,627 --> 00:01:36,007
But first, why the usual questions fail.

36
00:01:36,547 --> 00:01:38,847
Pitching your idea makes
the conversation about you.

37
00:01:39,469 --> 00:01:41,889
And asking "would you buy
this?", is asking about

38
00:01:41,909 --> 00:01:44,169
a future that costs
someone nothing to promise.

39
00:01:44,563 --> 00:01:47,423
Both of those invite a
kind person to be kind.

40
00:01:47,863 --> 00:01:49,823
it's the approach that
goes wrong, not the people.

41
00:01:50,323 --> 00:01:52,643
So the easy guesses get
tested, and the one that

42
00:01:52,663 --> 00:01:55,102
would kill the idea
waits until it's built.

43
00:01:55,362 --> 00:01:57,222
Rob Fitzpatrick, wrote The Mom Test.

44
00:01:57,602 --> 00:01:59,582
It says you should ask
about the past instead.

45
00:01:59,962 --> 00:02:02,202
Which is the same as looking
at what buyers already did.

46
00:02:03,094 --> 00:02:04,194
Take the example idea.

47
00:02:04,454 --> 00:02:07,514
Weekly planner paperbacks, printed by
Amazon only when someone orders one.

48
00:02:07,934 --> 00:02:10,714
The plan is a whole series, each
book made for one kind of person.

49
00:02:11,154 --> 00:02:13,114
It's a typical example,
you can find multiple

50
00:02:13,134 --> 00:02:14,978
youtube videos explaining how to do it.

51
00:02:15,238 --> 00:02:16,998
Underneath that sits a stack of guesses,

52
00:02:17,798 --> 00:02:19,878
like "freelancers search
Amazon for planners",

53
00:02:19,898 --> 00:02:22,858
and "they would pick a design
specifically made for them over a generic

54
00:02:22,878 --> 00:02:24,398
one". Each one feels true.

55
00:02:24,999 --> 00:02:27,399
Maybe one person even said
they wanted exactly that,

56
00:02:27,659 --> 00:02:29,239
but one anecdote is not proof.

57
00:02:29,699 --> 00:02:33,179
Those guesses are called assumptions:
what you believe about who has the

58
00:02:33,199 --> 00:02:35,819
problem, how badly, and
what they do about it today.

59
00:02:36,319 --> 00:02:39,779
Checking your assumptions before you
build it is customer discovery: finding

60
00:02:39,799 --> 00:02:42,819
out whether real people have the
problem and what they would pay for it.

61
00:02:43,413 --> 00:02:45,093
Like the business model in my last video,

62
00:02:45,633 --> 00:02:47,493
testing an idea should
be seen as a system.

63
00:02:48,053 --> 00:02:51,073
Time and access to customers run
out, so you can't test everything.

64
00:02:51,593 --> 00:02:53,713
The signal worth trusting,
is what people do.

65
00:02:54,333 --> 00:02:57,413
So your leverage point is the
riskiest guess that's cheap to test.

66
00:02:57,773 --> 00:03:00,453
If it's wrong, everything
resting on it cascades

67
00:03:00,473 --> 00:03:03,433
down with it: the product,
the price, the pitch.

68
00:03:04,273 --> 00:03:05,993
Two laws help you find that guess.

69
00:03:06,493 --> 00:03:08,853
Law one: test where change is cheapest.

70
00:03:09,313 --> 00:03:12,013
A flaw found before you
build costs a small test.

71
00:03:12,433 --> 00:03:14,513
But found after, it costs the whole build.

72
00:03:15,109 --> 00:03:17,489
Law two: measure behaviour, not opinion.

73
00:03:18,109 --> 00:03:21,409
Clayton Christensen told a story
about a fast-food chain and how people

74
00:03:21,429 --> 00:03:25,789
"hired" its milkshakes, which is his
jargon for "bringing a product into

75
00:03:25,809 --> 00:03:27,349
your life to get a job done".

76
00:03:27,817 --> 00:03:30,017
More shakes than you'd
expect sold early in the day,

77
00:03:30,277 --> 00:03:33,617
to commuters, who hired one to fill
a boring drive and keep hunger away.

78
00:03:34,277 --> 00:03:36,097
Parents, in the afternoon,
hired it for a different job.

79
00:03:36,693 --> 00:03:40,693
Christensen calls this jobs to be done:
people hire a product to make progress

80
00:03:40,713 --> 00:03:43,793
in a situation. The clue
was behaviour: when it sold,

81
00:03:44,073 --> 00:03:47,733
and who was buying. Find the job, and
the product, price and pitch follow.

82
00:03:48,445 --> 00:03:50,005
So where do you find that behaviour?

83
00:03:50,645 --> 00:03:52,405
Evidence comes like rungs on a ladder,

84
00:03:53,005 --> 00:03:56,565
cheapest first, and the cheapest
rung needs nobody to talk to.

85
00:03:56,945 --> 00:03:59,405
Rung one is existing
behaviour: what buyers

86
00:03:59,425 --> 00:04:01,945
of similar products already did and wrote.

87
00:04:02,253 --> 00:04:05,353
That used to mean going through
everything customers said, by hand.

88
00:04:06,033 --> 00:04:09,113
Now, a language model can read a pile
of reviews and group them into the

89
00:04:09,133 --> 00:04:11,953
struggles that keep coming back,
in the customers' exact words.

90
00:04:12,894 --> 00:04:16,014
Here's a real run on real reviews
of a planner already on sale.

91
00:04:16,654 --> 00:04:20,434
I pasted ten unhappy reviews into a free
Claude chat and asked for the situations

92
00:04:20,454 --> 00:04:23,594
people were in. The more you
give it, the more it can analyse.

93
00:04:24,374 --> 00:04:25,934
Claude came back with six situations.

94
00:04:26,617 --> 00:04:29,617
The three biggest each had three
reviews: planning the weekend,

95
00:04:30,057 --> 00:04:31,937
expecting more room
than it has, and a part

96
00:04:31,957 --> 00:04:33,881
failing from normal day to day use.

97
00:04:34,141 --> 00:04:35,621
Claude also flagged its own doubts.

98
00:04:36,221 --> 00:04:37,721
One review never mentions the weekend,

99
00:04:37,861 --> 00:04:40,921
so it was placed by inference, and
some reviews contradict each other,

100
00:04:41,101 --> 00:04:42,281
like thick paper against thin.

101
00:04:42,701 --> 00:04:45,841
That's why you spot-check three against
the originals: the sorting and counting

102
00:04:45,861 --> 00:04:47,461
are the model's work,
so they can be wrong.

103
00:04:48,054 --> 00:04:51,054
An unhappy review is a customer
describing the situation they were in.

104
00:04:51,434 --> 00:04:53,994
I've noticed this myself,
reading reviews before buying,

105
00:04:54,394 --> 00:04:56,074
that when a product
misses a big expectation,

106
00:04:56,474 --> 00:04:59,114
people usually explain why they're
frustrated, not just that it was bad.

107
00:04:59,742 --> 00:05:03,182
Research on vacation-rental reviews
found the same pattern: negative reviews

108
00:05:03,202 --> 00:05:05,222
tend to run long and
describe disappointment

109
00:05:05,242 --> 00:05:07,142
when expectations weren't met.

110
00:05:07,542 --> 00:05:10,522
What people write after a product
lets them down is closer to what they

111
00:05:10,542 --> 00:05:12,102
did than to just what they'd say.

112
00:05:12,991 --> 00:05:14,391
That's rung one of our ladder.

113
00:05:14,811 --> 00:05:18,191
Rung two goes further, because it
creates behaviour that doesn't exist yet.

114
00:05:18,611 --> 00:05:21,191
That's a smoke test. It's
a simple page offering a

115
00:05:21,211 --> 00:05:23,755
free sample of the product,
with an email sign-up.

116
00:05:24,015 --> 00:05:26,695
Then a small ad or a community
post sends people there.

117
00:05:27,115 --> 00:05:28,295
Now you can see what people do.

118
00:05:28,735 --> 00:05:30,295
AI builds the page and writes the ad.

119
00:05:30,655 --> 00:05:32,935
You put them live, set the
spend and read the count,

120
00:05:33,255 --> 00:05:34,695
because that count is your evidence.

121
00:05:35,394 --> 00:05:37,774
Before you start, you set a
pass line and a fail line.

122
00:05:38,134 --> 00:05:39,514
They're the counts that mean keep going,

123
00:05:39,794 --> 00:05:41,334
and the counts that mean stop and rethink.

124
00:05:41,874 --> 00:05:44,934
You set them first, so you can't
talk yourself into a pass afterwards.

125
00:05:45,623 --> 00:05:47,563
One caution to keep in
mind with the free tool.

126
00:05:48,003 --> 00:05:49,623
Here's my view, not research.

127
00:05:50,183 --> 00:05:52,483
An AI summary of reviews
is not an actual customer,

128
00:05:52,903 --> 00:05:54,243
and neither is an AI playing one.

129
00:05:55,003 --> 00:05:57,423
Use AI to read real words and to prepare,

130
00:05:57,803 --> 00:06:00,243
but the real evidence still
comes from what real people do.

131
00:06:00,643 --> 00:06:03,483
It gives you insight to what
people said, not what they will do.

132
00:06:04,378 --> 00:06:05,558
Back to the example idea.

133
00:06:06,058 --> 00:06:07,478
Here's the free guess finder tool.

134
00:06:07,958 --> 00:06:11,718
Two sentences go in: weekly planner
paperbacks for freelancers who work

135
00:06:11,738 --> 00:06:15,238
from home, plus the logic (or
rather beliefs) behind the idea.

136
00:06:15,923 --> 00:06:17,283
What comes back is the test card.

137
00:06:17,603 --> 00:06:20,743
It holds five guesses with a risk
score and a cost score for each,

138
00:06:21,263 --> 00:06:24,483
the ranked order, one test with
a pass line and a fail line,

139
00:06:24,663 --> 00:06:25,763
and how to read the result.

140
00:06:26,332 --> 00:06:29,512
The first guess is that home-working
freelancers have a week-organising

141
00:06:29,532 --> 00:06:32,152
problem bad enough that they'd
buy a paper planner for it.

142
00:06:32,532 --> 00:06:34,812
The other four cover
search, the specific design,

143
00:06:35,252 --> 00:06:37,032
repeat buying and how
crowded the space is.

144
00:06:37,699 --> 00:06:41,059
The first guess, demand, comes
back high risk and cheap to test.

145
00:06:41,690 --> 00:06:43,270
The ranking after demand is crowding,

146
00:06:43,490 --> 00:06:46,090
then the design claim, then
search, then repeat buying.

147
00:06:47,650 --> 00:06:49,450
You might expect the
design claim to come first,

148
00:06:49,570 --> 00:06:51,470
since it's the whole
pitch, but it ranks third,

149
00:06:51,670 --> 00:06:53,670
because it costs more to
test than demand or crowding.

150
00:06:54,418 --> 00:06:57,918
Demand beats crowding because crowding
only matters if demand exists.

151
00:06:58,438 --> 00:06:59,878
So the leverage point is demand.

152
00:07:00,398 --> 00:07:03,898
Do freelancers who work from home
really want a paper planner to fix their

153
00:07:03,918 --> 00:07:06,398
week? If that's wrong, the search, design

154
00:07:06,418 --> 00:07:09,238
and repeat-buying guesses fall with it.

155
00:07:09,498 --> 00:07:11,478
The test starts on rung one of the ladder,

156
00:07:11,898 --> 00:07:15,218
because this guess is about what
buyers already do and complain about.

157
00:07:15,718 --> 00:07:17,858
It's about two to three
hours of copying the reviews,

158
00:07:18,298 --> 00:07:19,958
or you can let AI collect them for you.

159
00:07:20,258 --> 00:07:22,098
Then it reads everything in one pass.

160
00:07:22,792 --> 00:07:23,972
Here's how you read the result.

161
00:07:24,472 --> 00:07:26,352
A pass means keep the guess and move on.

162
00:07:26,792 --> 00:07:29,492
A fail means change the idea,
the customer or the claim.

163
00:07:30,212 --> 00:07:31,832
Anything in between means narrow the test,

164
00:07:32,072 --> 00:07:33,752
make it cheaper or step up on the ladder.

165
00:07:34,432 --> 00:07:37,152
The tool also gives an honest
line, and I agree with it.

166
00:07:37,472 --> 00:07:39,372
No fully automated validation exists.

167
00:07:39,892 --> 00:07:42,712
A human has to act, but you don't
have to talk to anyone to start.

168
00:07:43,307 --> 00:07:45,827
So the answer to the question
from the start is behaviour.

169
00:07:46,427 --> 00:07:48,227
It starts with what buyers already did,

170
00:07:48,507 --> 00:07:50,567
and then moves to what they
do when you offer something.

171
00:07:51,283 --> 00:07:55,023
Your move this week: run the Riskiest
Guess Finder on your own idea.

172
00:07:55,503 --> 00:07:57,963
Fill in your pass and fail numbers
before you look at anything.

173
00:07:58,967 --> 00:08:02,287
If your test card gave you a test,
the next questions are how to run it

174
00:08:02,307 --> 00:08:03,907
and how to read what comes back.

175
00:08:04,467 --> 00:08:07,887
That's what the Business Idea Test
Kit adds: the Evidence Ladder Plan.

176
00:08:08,588 --> 00:08:11,788
So it follows the same ladder:
prompts to scan competitor reviews,

177
00:08:12,188 --> 00:08:15,948
a smoke-test page and ad prompts, and
templates for your pass and fail lines.

178
00:08:16,626 --> 00:08:19,406
If you can reach people, there's
an optional conversation guide too.

179
00:08:19,726 --> 00:08:21,286
It's for people who want to run the tests,

180
00:08:21,506 --> 00:08:23,886
not just plan them. The discount
code is in the description.

181
00:08:24,426 --> 00:08:27,366
If you just want to get an idea of the
risks of your business idea guesses,

182
00:08:27,746 --> 00:08:29,946
then it's perfectly fine to just
use the free guess finder tool.

183
00:08:30,612 --> 00:08:32,312
Here's what you should
take away from this video.

184
00:08:32,712 --> 00:08:34,632
Your idea is an unverified prototype,

185
00:08:34,952 --> 00:08:37,512
no matter how great you and the
people closest to you think it is.

186
00:08:37,792 --> 00:08:39,792
Test it on real customers
before you build it.

187
00:08:40,292 --> 00:08:42,532
Look at the behaviour of
your competitors' customers.

188
00:08:43,072 --> 00:08:46,392
The time spent verifying before building
is cheap compared to the time put

189
00:08:46,412 --> 00:08:50,152
in to make and then remake it (or having
to throw it all away after finding

190
00:08:50,172 --> 00:08:51,272
no customer uptake).

191
00:08:51,818 --> 00:08:53,518
The Riskiest Guess Finder tool is free,

192
00:08:53,878 --> 00:08:55,838
the link to get it emailed
to you is in the description.

193
00:08:56,458 --> 00:08:59,178
If people aren't willing to pay,
there's nothing worth building yet.

194
00:08:59,558 --> 00:09:00,658
Think about that for a moment.

195
00:09:01,060 --> 00:09:03,840
The cheapest time to find out you're
wrong is always before you build.

196
00:09:04,340 --> 00:09:05,560
Friends agreeing isn't evidence.

197
00:09:05,860 --> 00:09:06,980
Strangers acting is.

198
00:09:07,400 --> 00:09:09,580
Tell me in the comments:
what's the one guess your

199
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:11,656
idea depends on that you've never tested?

